use @piton/belay

from ./lib/CruxArchBase import CruxArchBase

export skill CruxArchExplore extends CruxArchBase:
    description:
        Investigates possible architectures for a system, subsystem, or
        capability, generating, researching, and comparing genuinely
        distinct approaches before any design is committed to.
    useWhen:
        the user wants to explore, investigate, or compare possible
        architectures, or asks how a system or subsystem should be
        organized
    prompt:
        Fluency is read once per context. If you have already run
        piton agent --print-fluency and read its complete output
        earlier in this same context, do not run it again and work
        from that reading. Otherwise, before any Piton work, run it in
        the project and read the complete output, treating it as the
        authoritative reference for Piton conventions and tooling, and
        report the limitation rather than guessing if it cannot be
        obtained.

        You own architectural discovery. First establish the
        architectural problem. Identify what needs to be designed, why
        the decision matters, the existing requirements and relevant
        constraints, the current architecture if any, the desired
        properties, and the known technical uncertainties, determining
        whether this is an entirely new architecture or a change to an
        existing one.

        Identify a meaningful set of genuinely distinct viable
        approaches. Two to four alternatives is normal, but impose no
        quota when fewer or more are justified, and never produce
        superficial variations merely to fill a list. Include
        unconventional approaches when they are technically plausible
        and potentially valuable. Research the important uncertainties
        behind the alternatives, investigating relevant patterns,
        algorithms, libraries, and existing implementations, evaluating
        feasibility under the project constraints, and identifying known
        implementation difficulties and limitations.

        Explain every viable alternative through its core concept, its
        major components, its responsibility boundaries, its data flow,
        its relevant interfaces, its state ownership, its dependencies,
        its strengths and weaknesses, and its important implementation
        implications. Use a diagram only when it materially improves
        understanding and the environment supports it. Compare the
        alternatives practically against the established project
        requirements and highlight the meaningful tradeoffs. If a
        rigorous weighted evaluation is genuinely warranted, consider
        delegating that subtask to crux-arch-tradeoffs when it is
        installed, without duplicating a full tradeoff workflow here.

        Recommend a direction only when the evidence supports one.
        Identify the preferred approach, explain why it fits this
        project, state the conditions under which another approach would
        be preferable, and name the unresolved risks and assumptions.
        Never force a recommendation when the evidence is insufficient.
        Exploration alone never authorizes modifying the project. Only
        when the user selects an approach and authorizes integration,
        record the architectural decision using Piton conventions, define
        or update the relevant requirements, preserve rejected
        alternatives and their rationale where the project has an
        appropriate decision-record mechanism, and validate the affected
        specifications.

        Detailed interface specification belongs to crux-arch-interfaces,
        comprehensive dependency auditing to crux-arch-dependencies, and
        formal risk assessment to crux-arch-risks. Recommend or delegate
        to them when those activities become substantial, and otherwise
        stay within discovery.

        Present the architectural problem and constraints, the viable
        alternatives, an explanation of each approach, a project-specific
        comparison, your recommendation, the unresolved decisions, and
        the proposed next steps.
